# Every part as filed, resolved or not. One row per (composition, composed layer, part): the raw
# incidence matrix F, before anybody has checked that the thing it points at exists.
#
# An incidence entry and a conversion factor at once
#   A part is both, which is why F and Φ live in one table. The entry says which layer of which
#   document goes into which layer of the composer's own; the factor says what to multiply it by
#   to reach that layer's unit.
#
# A part names its target by a notation
#   Not by a filename. A row resolves only where a document declares its own notation, so the
#   conformance rule "a dependence end's filing exists, and the layer named is in it" presupposes
#   that lookup: a foreign key needs something to point at.
#
# The factor's state, assigned once
#   `asrt:Part/factor` is optional and its choice has three arms, so a part files its conversion in
#   one of four ways, which `public.factor_state` declares: omitted (the units already agree, and the
#   factor is one), stated, absent, or a derivation. Three nullable columns carry them, and every
#   reader asking which kind of factor a part has asks this column rather than testing the three
#   again. `pm.part`'s CHECKs let at most one of the columns be set, so the arms cannot overlap and
#   the omitted arm is exactly the part with none of them. algebra/factor_state.sqlc holds each
#   part's state to the column it names.
-- asrt:Composition/asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part, keyed by pm:ForeignId (notation + id).
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, p.part_regime,
       p.factor_low, p.factor_mode, p.factor_high, p.factor_absent, p.factor_derivation,
       CASE WHEN p.factor_low        IS NOT NULL THEN 'stated'::public.factor_state
            WHEN p.factor_absent     IS NOT NULL THEN 'absent'::public.factor_state
            WHEN p.factor_derivation IS NOT NULL THEN 'derivation'::public.factor_state
            ELSE                                      'omitted'::public.factor_state END AS factor_state,
       p.part_party, p.part_registration_taxonomy, p.part_registration_value, p.part_version
FROM pm.part p
